k  or you can write in the chat so maybe i  can start with a very simple question so  if i just think of the non-probabilistic  reactive synthesis setting  uh does this kind of does this give you  a way of doing abstract reactive  synthesis right where you kind of  learn strategies for you know some  particular things and then you put them  together  in a way so you know you're working in  the world of mark of decision processes  but does this  imply something about hierarchical  reactive synthesis  um i mean the theory does hold  yeah i mean in the pure automata all the  theory were  right i mean it doesn't like any fixed  deterministic process is also an  also an ndp uh  but i think um what  this is essentially saying is  is if you could identify uh sort of  key points that you want to uh visit  during your  uh during your execution  then that is good enough and you you  have some sort of  [Music]  you can abstract away the problem into a  much smaller  simpler finite state problems even if  your underlying thing is continuous  but if your underlying thing is finite  even i think  you can still do this if if the state  space is large you could  you might want to reduce it to a smaller  state space by doing this sort of  approach  but that setting is not something that  we studied and  yeah and maybe it's a good direction for  future work  thank you other questions  so if i may just ask a follow-up so  these abstract states are  so you said that at this point you kind  of  so the user has to provide them yeah but  in your experiments you  just tried some random points as  intermediate abstract points is that  right uh not in these not in the primary  experiments but we  also considered randomly generated  rooms environments i see so in the  primary experiments essentially the  structure of the problem the rooms  already gave you a natural mapping to  abstract states correct  yeah and in in this case of ant we just  we sort of know what the environment  looks like s